WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.720
 If you've been reading the news this week or perhaps walking outside, you would know

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:11.060
 that Europe is absolutely boiling.

00:00:11.060 --> 00:00:16.500
 And the people living under this heat dome, which is hovering over much of Western Europe,

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:19.880
 are struggling to cope.

00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:25.660
 In London, teachers spray children with a hose to help cool down in the blistering heat.

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:31.380
 And Prague beleaguered horses cool off in a river. In Paris, overheated shoppers line

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:37.020
 up to buy fans.

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:44.720
 Tereen Lebousit says the 100-degree heat wave has been "hell on earth."

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:49.520
 These are the scenes this week across Europe, from Bordeaux to Barcelona.

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:55.960
 The continent has endured a brutal, record-smashing heat wave that has turned deadly.

00:00:55.960 --> 00:01:02.540
 France saw a huge spike in drownings as people tried to beat the heat in the water.

00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:07.980
 And if you feel like this kind of weather is new, abnormal, you're right.

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:13.880
 And if there are any doubt, we've just launched the Reuters Climate Monitor, which is an interactive

00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:20.000
 map that tracks just how abnormal the current high temperatures are across the globe.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:25.760
 So on today's On Assignment, we'll sit down with Ben Welsh, the data journalist behind

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:32.160
 the project, to break down what the data actually shows, how unusual this moment really is,

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:38.040
 and what it tells us about where this summer, and our climate, is headed next.

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:39.760
 I'm your host, Jonah Green.

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:54.780
 Ben Welsh is an investigative journalist who focuses on data-driven reporting and newsroom

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:55.880
 tools.

00:01:55.880 --> 00:01:58.480
 And he joins us here today on the show.

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:00.760
 Thanks for coming on, Ben.

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:01.760
 Thanks for having me.

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:02.760
 I'm excited to be here.

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:04.720
 That's the spirit.

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:07.140
 Where are you currently?

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:09.720
 Is it also very hot where you are?

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:15.560
 I'm talking to you right now from Money Making Manhattan, the center of it all, where I work

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:20.280
 many days in the Times Square newsroom, but I'm coming to you from my home here on 28th

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:21.280
 Street.

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:22.280
 It looks nice.

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:25.120
 It's a sticky, warm day that we have here today in New York.

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:30.000
 I wouldn't call it pleasant, but it's not as dangerous as the heat we're reporting on

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:31.280
 in other parts of the world.

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:32.280
 Yeah.

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:38.560
 I mean, it is, I was in New York a few days ago, it was oddly cool, which was a weird contrast

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:46.240
 because New York City, especially, is no stranger to these disgustingly hot, humid summers.

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:53.120
 And it's not uncommon for it to be 100 degrees Fahrenheit, which is 37 centigrade.

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:57.120
 But Europe seems to be having a really hard time with these numbers.

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:05.240
 And as you see the death toll coming out of these countries, why is Europe struggling

00:03:05.240 --> 00:03:07.120
 so much with this kind of heat?

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:11.920
 Well, currently, there's a high pressure system, not to get too nerdy right away out the gate,

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:17.560
 but there's a dome, there's a kind of air trapped over Europe, what's called an Omega

00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:18.560
 wave, I'm told.

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:22.880
 Or it's really just a big bubble of hot air that has dried out the area and has brought

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:27.960
 those temperatures up to highs that are rarely seen in that part of the world.

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:32.080
 And there's one forecaster in France who's saying it's the highest temperature ever recorded.

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:36.000
 We'll see if that holds up as people scrub the data in the coming days.

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:37.000
 But it's really hot.

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:41.760
 You might live in a place where 90 degrees Fahrenheit doesn't seem that hot to you.

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:45.540
 And I think it's the to you and to them that's the key piece of it.

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:52.680
 Because heat is dangerous for absolute reasons to talk in a mathy way, a high number is a

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:53.680
 high number.

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:56.320
 But it's also relative to where you live.

00:03:56.320 --> 00:03:59.640
 And that has to do with the infrastructure of where you live.

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:01.480
 Do you have air conditioning?

00:04:01.480 --> 00:04:02.480
 Yes or no.

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:07.320
 And also kind of cultural factors having to do with people's lifestyle, do they spend

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:13.840
 a lot of time indoors or outdoors and do they are they practiced at protecting themselves

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:16.080
 from heat?

00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:20.840
 Why is El Nino so powerful this year?

00:04:20.840 --> 00:04:21.840
 Sure.

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:26.240
 So El Nino is a cyclical thing that's been going on long before you and I are born.

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:33.000
 Basically, every few years, the sea temperature, the heat in the water, you know, in the eastern

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:38.200
 Pacific kind of by Peru and Central America, just south of the United States and Mexico

00:04:38.200 --> 00:04:39.800
 will heat up.

00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:46.320
 And that process, which is about every two to seven years, has a ripple effect that goes

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:50.600
 off across the entire globe, leading to lots and lots of different changes to our weather

00:04:50.600 --> 00:04:55.120
 that have become fairly predictable as scientists have tested it and measured it through the

00:04:55.120 --> 00:04:56.120
 years.

00:04:56.120 --> 00:04:59.600
 And one thing it does is it leads to a weaker hurricane season, typically, though you still

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:02.320
 can have dangerous hurricanes in an El Nino year.

00:05:02.320 --> 00:05:09.360
 It leads to more rainfall in Southern California and at maybe the most global level, it tends

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:13.880
 to lead to higher temperatures all across the planet and in a few particular places

00:05:13.880 --> 00:05:14.960
 as well.

00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:21.400
 And so as of this week, the U.S. Weather Service is saying we are entering our first El Nino

00:05:21.400 --> 00:05:22.720
 in several years.

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:26.480
 It's you know, no one can know precisely how big it will be.

00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:31.480
 But due to some of the trends we've seen over the last few decades, the expectation and

00:05:31.480 --> 00:05:36.480
 the forecast is it will be one of the largest on record, which some are dubbing the super

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:38.000
 El Nino.

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:44.600
 If that comes to pass, it's predicted that we may see the hottest months and years on

00:05:44.600 --> 00:05:48.600
 record as a result in the days ahead.

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:53.800
 And making people uncomfortable or causing death in certain cases, for those who are

00:05:53.800 --> 00:05:55.800
 more vulnerable.

00:05:55.800 --> 00:06:02.080
 What does this kind of heat do, you know, globally to supply chains, food production,

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:03.240
 migratory patterns?

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:09.360
 Like, give us a sense of the disruption that we may not even be aware of.

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:14.800
 Yeah, I mean, it's just like climate change itself, it can be a slow and difficult to

00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:19.360
 directly observe kind of patterns, but we know that there's all kinds of shifts to where

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:25.720
 people live, how they're living, agriculture, how the natural ecosystem and other animals

00:06:25.720 --> 00:06:31.520
 besides humans are living and moving, all kind of adds up to these larger scale changes

00:06:31.520 --> 00:06:36.000
 over time that we expect to unfold.

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:43.200
 And it's hard to it's hard to directly measure that or understand and see all of it happening.

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:49.200
 But as the global climate system changes, the world will evolve and adapt to match that

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:50.200
 gradually.

00:06:50.200 --> 00:06:57.240
 And so a lot of us just kind of feel like it's hotter than we remember, and I think

00:06:57.240 --> 00:06:59.320
 the data bears that out.

00:06:59.320 --> 00:07:05.200
 But hopefully you have put together this database for where it is called the Climate Monitor

00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:12.160
 to visualize just how much hotter it is compared to historic averages.

00:07:12.160 --> 00:07:17.320
 Tell me about this project and how it works and why it came about.

00:07:17.320 --> 00:07:20.680
 Sure, I think you framed it pretty well there.

00:07:20.680 --> 00:07:25.800
 I can't speak for everyone watching and listening, but I suspect that many people out there in

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:31.800
 Listenerland have woken up to a high weather forecast in the area where they lived and

00:07:31.800 --> 00:07:36.520
 thought to themselves, "Hey, has it always been this hot here this time of year?

00:07:36.520 --> 00:07:38.600
 Could it be climate change?

00:07:38.600 --> 00:07:41.000
 Is this weird?"

00:07:41.000 --> 00:07:44.080
 And I think that more and more people are asking themselves that question on a day-to-day

00:07:44.080 --> 00:07:47.840
 basis, and we wanted to try to help people find the answer.

00:07:47.840 --> 00:07:52.160
 And so what we've done is we've created a new website called the Reuters Climate Monitor,

00:07:52.160 --> 00:07:58.040
 which is it updates daily and it takes the weather forecast for everywhere on Earth,

00:07:58.040 --> 00:08:03.520
 and it compares today's high temperature to what used to be normal in that same place

00:08:03.520 --> 00:08:08.280
 about a century ago with kind of the oldest available data that we have and what a climate

00:08:08.280 --> 00:08:11.440
 scientist would call the "normal temperature."

00:08:11.440 --> 00:08:16.120
 And so you're able to on any given day say, "Here's the high now, and here's what the

00:08:16.120 --> 00:08:19.320
 high typically used to be in the same place."

00:08:19.320 --> 00:08:23.520
 That's plotted out on the map so you can see patterns across the whole world, and it wouldn't

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:28.360
 surprise you if you were to look at it today and you were following the news to see that

00:08:28.360 --> 00:08:33.680
 there's a big red spot over Europe right now where the temperatures are highly unusual for

00:08:33.680 --> 00:08:36.880
 this time of year in those areas.

00:08:36.880 --> 00:08:41.280
 And just to give you some specifics, you can look at some of the cities in northern Spain

00:08:41.280 --> 00:08:46.420
 on the Atlantic coast, which are actually kind of the cooler part of Spain historically,

00:08:46.420 --> 00:08:50.840
 are seeing highs come in this week that are nearly double what the temperature kind of

00:08:50.840 --> 00:08:57.160
 typically was in those same places between 1960 and 1990, which is the comparison period

00:08:57.160 --> 00:08:58.160
 that we use.

00:08:58.160 --> 00:08:59.160
 That's unbelievable.

00:08:59.160 --> 00:09:05.680
 Yeah, I was playing with it earlier, and it's pretty shocking just to see it laid out so

00:09:05.680 --> 00:09:10.860
 clearly to show that yes, the world is getting hotter than it used to be.

00:09:10.860 --> 00:09:15.200
 It's not our imagination, and you can see that very clearly on the graph.

00:09:15.200 --> 00:09:20.720
 Let's see, I'm going to try to put in Paris here to see what this brings up.

00:09:20.720 --> 00:09:29.960
 Yeah, today's high forecast is 100 degrees Fahrenheit, which is 38 degrees in centigrade.

00:09:29.960 --> 00:09:36.840
 That is way above normal, and if you zoom in on the graph next to it, you could see

00:09:36.840 --> 00:09:42.540
 every month the temperature average is above normal.

00:09:42.540 --> 00:09:48.240
 This month so far it's 12 degrees, and then you have this nifty graph below kind of showing

00:09:48.240 --> 00:09:55.720
 the noise of many years past, but the blue line represents a normal average year, and

00:09:55.720 --> 00:09:59.840
 the red is this year, which is very high.

00:09:59.840 --> 00:10:04.440
 That chart shows you the entire globe in contrast to just Paris, but when you zoom in on any

00:10:04.440 --> 00:10:10.200
 place you can get the same comparison against a normal year for more than two million locations

00:10:10.200 --> 00:10:16.400
 on the planet using the data set that we've taken from the European Climate Agency.

00:10:16.400 --> 00:10:19.200
 How did you compile this?

00:10:19.200 --> 00:10:24.960
 We built it entirely using open source data created by the European Weather Agency.

00:10:24.960 --> 00:10:29.480
 American listeners and watchers may be familiar with the National Weather Service, which is

00:10:29.480 --> 00:10:33.100
 the United States kind of government-funded forecasting unit.

00:10:33.100 --> 00:10:39.320
 There's a similar operation in Europe that actually has what scientists will tell you

00:10:39.320 --> 00:10:45.160
 is the most authoritative record of Earth's temperatures for not just the planet as a

00:10:45.160 --> 00:10:51.280
 whole but for any location on Earth going back to 1940, and that database is the core

00:10:51.280 --> 00:10:57.880
 and the foundation of almost all climate science, but because it is generated using supercomputers

00:10:57.880 --> 00:11:05.620
 and is highly sophisticated, it takes five days for it to generate today's temperatures.

00:11:05.620 --> 00:11:09.160
 Today's temperatures won't enter that data set until five days from now.

00:11:09.160 --> 00:11:14.920
 The result of that has been that almost all climate science and reporting that's done,

00:11:14.920 --> 00:11:19.280
 not just in the news media but in the scientific community, tends to have a delay in it.

00:11:19.280 --> 00:11:24.680
 You're usually hearing about the high temperature last month or last week or last year, and

00:11:24.680 --> 00:11:29.320
 it's often delivered you in kind of the arcane statistics of an average temperature.

00:11:29.320 --> 00:11:32.560
 I doubt there's anyone out there that wakes up in the morning and asks themselves, "What

00:11:32.560 --> 00:11:34.960
 will the average temperature be today?"

00:11:34.960 --> 00:11:38.680
 That number is really useful when you're trying to look at the global trend at the highest

00:11:38.680 --> 00:11:42.760
 possible level, but it doesn't help the average person get the context that they're looking

00:11:42.760 --> 00:11:46.840
 for on the life that they're living or to help them better understand what's going on

00:11:46.840 --> 00:11:49.680
 in Europe right now if they're not there.

00:11:49.680 --> 00:11:53.840
 What we've tried to do is bridge that gap by taking today's weather forecast put out

00:11:53.840 --> 00:11:57.640
 by the same agency and doing a simple comparison of those two.

00:11:57.640 --> 00:12:02.740
 Now, there's some statistical procedures you go through to take two different models and

00:12:02.740 --> 00:12:03.740
 compare them.

00:12:03.740 --> 00:12:07.200
 We interviewed and worked with some of the world's leading climate scientists to make

00:12:07.200 --> 00:12:13.760
 sure we got all the math right and did it the correct way, but the result is by taking

00:12:13.760 --> 00:12:18.240
 today's forecast and comparing it to that historic record, we're able to do in real

00:12:18.240 --> 00:12:25.340
 time and ahead of time tell you how unusual today's temperatures are for any place on

00:12:25.340 --> 00:12:26.340
 Earth.

00:12:26.340 --> 00:12:32.400
 That's the most novel thing scientifically in our Humble project is putting those two

00:12:32.400 --> 00:12:37.880
 data sets together from the same agency in this new way.

00:12:37.880 --> 00:12:47.660
 This heat wave, this El Nino, this supercharged heat, and this aberration of average temperatures,

00:12:47.660 --> 00:12:49.280
 this is climate change.

00:12:49.280 --> 00:12:50.280
 Yes.

00:12:50.280 --> 00:12:53.640
 Well, what is climate change is a wonderful philosophical question.

00:12:53.640 --> 00:12:57.560
 I think it means different things to different people over time.

00:12:57.560 --> 00:13:03.160
 One thing a lot of experts emphasize is that the word climate change was selected and is

00:13:03.160 --> 00:13:06.360
 popularized because it has a broad application.

00:13:06.360 --> 00:13:11.280
 It can mean things getting hotter and in some cases maybe things getting colder or other

00:13:11.280 --> 00:13:17.160
 parts of the climate changing besides temperature, looking at other things that go on with the

00:13:17.160 --> 00:13:21.600
 atmosphere and in our oceans.

00:13:21.600 --> 00:13:25.680
 Climate change broadly is used to encompass all of those things.

00:13:25.680 --> 00:13:30.240
 I think that what we're primarily measuring thus far, we do want to add a lot of stuff

00:13:30.240 --> 00:13:36.240
 in the future we haven't got to yet, but what we're primarily focusing on so far is really

00:13:36.240 --> 00:13:41.040
 what used to be called more commonly, you don't hear as much anymore as global warming,

00:13:41.040 --> 00:13:46.720
 which is just the planet gradually getting warmer over time, which there is just overwhelming

00:13:46.720 --> 00:13:53.120
 evidence for and the data sets we're bringing to bear here are ones that we hope will try

00:13:53.120 --> 00:13:59.240
 to help the average person better see and understand what the numbers have to tell us.

00:13:59.240 --> 00:14:00.240
 Yeah.

00:14:00.240 --> 00:14:01.720
 What does a data reporter do?

00:14:01.720 --> 00:14:05.800
 It's a great question, I wish I got asked it more often.

00:14:05.800 --> 00:14:07.720
 What does a data reporter do?

00:14:07.720 --> 00:14:16.400
 I would say that we use STEM, science, technology, math skills to find and tell stories.

00:14:16.400 --> 00:14:23.200
 And that could mean downloading a spreadsheet and analyzing it and sorting it and then seeing

00:14:23.200 --> 00:14:27.280
 who's in the number one spot on the list and giving them a phone call.

00:14:27.280 --> 00:14:32.900
 And so it's about, to me, kind of a smarter or more scientific form of really traditional

00:14:32.900 --> 00:14:34.600
 journalistic techniques.

00:14:34.600 --> 00:14:40.480
 And so we're trying to use our technical skills to, like I said, find and tell stories.

00:14:40.480 --> 00:14:45.160
 So like another example on our desk just today, which is Tuesday, is we have a new public

00:14:45.160 --> 00:14:51.160
 opinion poll from our partner at Ipsos on how Americans feel about the president's performance

00:14:51.160 --> 00:14:54.500
 and the outcome thus far of the war in Iran.

00:14:54.500 --> 00:14:58.520
 And that, you know, public opinion polling is a statistical sample that we're doing and

00:14:58.520 --> 00:15:04.000
 then trying to find the story out of the headline and bring it across to our readers.

00:15:04.000 --> 00:15:08.080
 And a fun thing about being a data reporter is because you're a specialist and you have

00:15:08.080 --> 00:15:13.640
 some of those skills that help you do things that maybe not everybody knows how to do,

00:15:13.640 --> 00:15:15.660
 but you're able to cover a lot of different topics.

00:15:15.660 --> 00:15:20.040
 And so a fun thing about being a data reporter is you can cover public opinion one day, you

00:15:20.040 --> 00:15:25.960
 can cover economics another, and you can cover climate the next.

00:15:25.960 --> 00:15:35.680
 And it seems especially valuable now, if I may, given the kind of divisive political

00:15:35.680 --> 00:15:41.000
 scene we have here, especially in the U.S. and the fragmented media landscape in which,

00:15:41.000 --> 00:15:47.860
 you know, various news agencies can sort of portray their own reality.

00:15:47.860 --> 00:15:54.520
 And so, but you are, you are operating, at least the goal here is within the realm of

00:15:54.520 --> 00:15:56.720
 the objective.

00:15:56.720 --> 00:15:58.520
 That's the goal.

00:15:58.520 --> 00:16:01.160
 The reader can be the judge on how well I'm doing.

00:16:01.160 --> 00:16:06.000
 And I welcome their critiques and, you know, obviously just to say it, numbers and statistics

00:16:06.000 --> 00:16:09.440
 can and have very frequently been used to mislead people, you know?

00:16:09.440 --> 00:16:15.560
 And so they are, you know, not unimpeachable or undefeated on the field of play.

00:16:15.560 --> 00:16:20.380
 But as someone who's just trying to learn new things every day and share those things

00:16:20.380 --> 00:16:25.440
 with our readers to try to be able to verify what we're doing and convince people that

00:16:25.440 --> 00:16:33.760
 they should maybe consider learning from it, you know, showing your work, being clear and

00:16:33.760 --> 00:16:40.280
 verifiable, you know, and hopefully convincing is what data can help you do.

00:16:40.280 --> 00:16:45.840
 And it doesn't mean every data, every data analysis follows that or achieves that.

00:16:45.840 --> 00:16:50.200
 But it's a tool that we use to try to do better journalism.

00:16:50.200 --> 00:16:54.000
 Check out the website, guys, Reuters Climate Monitor, send me your bug reports.

00:16:54.000 --> 00:16:55.000
 Yeah.

00:16:55.000 --> 00:17:07.040
 Let me put all your complaints in the YouTube comments.

00:17:07.040 --> 00:17:09.600
 Thanks again to Ben for his time in reporting.

